#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125740 :tutorial/content "The same property holds true in the case of three or more variables. In general, if $f$ is a differentiable real-valued function of $n$ variables, $\\mathbf y$ is a point in the domain of $f,$ and if  the  gradient vector is nonzero, i.e., \n\n$$\n\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)= \\left \\langle f_{x_1}(\\mathbf y), f_{x_2}(\\mathbf y), \\cdots, f_{x_n}(\\mathbf y)\\right \\rangle \\neq \\mathbf 0,\n$$\n\nthen we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)$ is a normal vector to the level surface $f(x_1,x_2, \\cdots, x_n) =f(\\mathbf y)$ at $\\mathbf y.$*\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function  \n\n$$\nw = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2-1\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $\\mathcal S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 +z^2 =1.$ \n\nThe point $P(0,0,1)$ lies on $\\mathcal S.$ Computing $\\nabla f(0,0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) &= \\langle f_x, \\: f_y, \\:f_z\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12\\langle2x, \\: 2y, \\: 2z\\rangle\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\langle x, \\: y, \\: z\\rangle\\\\[10pt]\n\n\\nabla f(0,0,1) &= \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1 \\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow clearly, $\\nabla f (0,0, 1)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathcal S$ at the point $(0,0,1).$\n\n![](images/88/88bf25de31ae3318379865cdf2590c15216cb984cbf3948f975caa47317be664.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "86223826-30df-5eba-9375-355401199095" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2524 :tutorial/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector For Functions of More than Two Variables"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125754 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we may need to simplify a limit before we can evaluate it. \n\nFor example, let's consider the limit\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2}.\n$$\n\nDirect substitution causes this limit to take the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 00$ because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = \\dfrac{(2)^2 + 2(2)(-1)}{(2)(-1) + 2(-1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling a common factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy}{xy+2{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{y(x+2y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y} &=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = -2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8f5c0d1f-e38c-5cd7-97e2-1aaaac5158b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2883 :tutorial/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125806 :tutorial/content "For a multivariable function, the existence of the gradient implies continuity.\n\nA sufficient condition for the gradient $\\nabla f(\\mathbf a)$ to exist is that the partial derivatives $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ both exist in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf a$ and are continuous at $\\mathbf a$.\n\nTherefore, if $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ both exist in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf a$ and are continuous at $\\mathbf a$, then $f$ is continuous at $\\mathbf a.$\n\n**Watch out!** The continuity of the partial derivatives is crucial. The existence of the partial derivatives alone does not imply that a function is continuous at a point." :tutorial/id #uuid "652f49b9-1a78-58d1-9531-1e115634f86a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3864 :tutorial/title "Continuous Partial Derivatives Imply Continuity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125807 :tutorial/content "Now, let's state the previous result more generally.\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be a function that's continuously differentiable at the point $P(x_0,y_0).$   If  $\\nabla f(x_0, y_0)$ is not the zero vector, then it is orthogonal to the tangent line to the level curve \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= f(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nat the point $P.$ The equation of the tangent can then be written as\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\! f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0,y-y_0\\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nWriting this explicitly, we get\n\n$$\nf_x(x_0,y_0)(x-x_0) + f_y(x_0,y_0)(y-y_0)   = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e9c2ee0b-08cf-5f42-bf09-f80dcd82051d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3866 :tutorial/title "Tangent Line to a Level Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125809 :tutorial/content "However, it is often easy to show that a limit does not exist for a multivariable function. All we have to do is find two paths on which the limit evaluates to different results. \n\nAs an example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)} \\dfrac{y}{x+y}\n$$\n\nClearly, direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00.$ Let's compute the limit along the paths $y=x$ and $y=x^2.$ We will see that the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\frac{y}{x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{x+x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{1+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{y}{x +y} = \\text{DNE}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8040690e-6d4c-50ac-8b9f-f92615717816" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3960 :tutorial/title "Proving That a Limit Does Not Exist by Evaluating It Along Different Paths"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129187 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129006} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d56cec4a-6121-5988-aaea-478fb6048a6d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian Determinant of a Transformation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129197 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129015} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da85f713-e8f7-5e04-b9e6-b1b33383be9f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Area by Transforming to a Type I Region"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129261 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128836} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129262 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128837} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c444d700-8c19-5712-8cf5-99555938b0c5" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Not Given"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129263 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128838} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab643519-92a4-5ccf-b826-fb2a68b36534" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Jacobian Determinant"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129269 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128846} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129270 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128847} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}]}
